# A format hole typed by any type that crosses text

**Command** (from `engine_repros/`, compiler at avra-services `722523899`):

    AVRA_PROGRAM_NET=0 AVRA_CACHE_DIR=$(mktemp -d) timeout 100 /Users/tristan/projects/tristanMatthias/avra-services/build/avra run format_hole_any_text_type

**Today** (exit 1; paths shortened):

    error[build.failed]: `grammar Item` reads `{id}` as `IdeaId`, and a hole reads text as `string` or `int`
       ╭─[…/format_hole_any_text_type/src/main.av:15:1]
    15 │ grammar Item = "/items/{id: IdeaId}"
    error[type.format_parse]: `{id: IdeaId}` reads its span as `IdeaId`, and a hole reads text as `string` or `int`
       ╭─[…/src/main.av:19:9]
    help: take the span as text and convert it in the arm
    error[type.unknown_prop]: no property `v` on `string`
       ╭─[…/src/main.av:19:45]

(The third error is a follow-on: the refused hole falls back to binding `string`.)

**Wanted:** exit 0, printing `matched 7 miss 7 /items/i7`. The match arm binds
`id: IdeaId` through `IdeaId.from_text_form`. `Item.parse` builds the record
the same way, and `Item.print` writes the hole with `text_form()`.

**Where the choice is made today.** `features/formats/format.av` `reads_of`
matches the type's NAME as text: `match ty.name { "string" -> .Text, "int" ->
.Int, _ -> null }`. `check.av` and `builders.av` turn a null into the two
refusals above. STRINGS_LANDING §1.5 promised a `CaptureRow` registry, with
`int` as its first row and user types joining by adding a row. It also
deferred a trait, because `Self?` did not resolve in a trait method then.
Neither the registry nor the trait was built.

**Proposed protocol: one that already exists.** `@std/text`'s
`TextForm { fn text_form() -> string; static fn from_text_form(text: string) -> Self? }`
(`std-text/src/text.av:436`). Static trait methods now work. `@std/time`'s
`Time` already implements it, and std-validate's `texted` reads it (by name,
`implements(n, "TextForm")`). Proposal:
- A hole of type `T` reads through `T.from_text_form`; a null means the arm or
  the parse fails.
- `print` writes the hole with `text_form()`, and the round-trip check runs
  on that text.
- `string` and `int` stay the built-in readings, so `int` keeps its fast
  inline path.
- The refusal for any other type becomes "`T` does not implement `TextForm`".

**Law:** a hole reads its span as any type that crosses as text, and writes it
back the same way, so parse and print use one protocol per type.

**Second user:** a command-line argument parser that reads `--at {when: Time}`
through the same `TextForm` that JSON and form fields already use.
// A hole typed by a user type that crosses text through `@std/text`'s
// `TextForm` should parse through it and print through it.
use @std.text.{TextForm}

type IdeaId = { v: int }

impl TextForm for IdeaId {
    fn text_form() -> string { "i${self.v}" }
    static fn from_text_form(text: string) -> IdeaId? {
        if !text.starts_with("i") { return null }
        IdeaId { v: 7 }
    }
}

grammar Item = "/items/{id: IdeaId}"

fn arm(t: string) -> string {
    match t {
        "/items/{id: IdeaId}" -> "matched ${id.v}",
        _ -> "miss",
    }
}

let parsed = Item.parse("/items/i7")
let printed = Item.print(Item { id: IdeaId { v: 7 } }) ?? "refused"
"${arm("/items/i7")} ${arm("/items/x")} ${parsed!.id.v} ${printed}"
